The code smells is a `Multiply-Nested Container`. It is caused by the excessive use of triple nested list comprehensions in the `World` class constructor. This makes the code hard to read and understand. 

The refactoring technique to apply to this code is `Decompose Container`. This involves breaking down the complex nested containers into simpler ones, making the code more readable and maintainable.

Here is the refactored code:

```python
class Location:
    """A map cell, with the description that appears in the scene."""

    def __init__(self, description):
        self.description = description

    def get_description(self):
        return self.description


class World:
    """Map built from the descriptions: descriptions[x][y][z].

    The size on each axis comes from the first element; if the rows have
    different sizes, the map is irregular.
    """

    def __init__(self, descriptions):
        self.world_map = self.build_world_map(descriptions)
        self.world_map_size_x, self.world_map_size_y, self.world_map_size_z = self.get_world_map_size(descriptions)

    def build_world_map(self, descriptions):
        return [[[Location(text) for text in row] for row in plane] for plane in descriptions]

    def get_world_map_size(self, descriptions):
        first_plane = descriptions[0]
        return len(descriptions), len(first_plane), len(first_plane[0])

    def get_location(self, x, y, z):
        plane = self.world_map[x]
        return plane[y][z]


def render_scene(world):
    """Render the current scene."""
    print("\n" * 100)

    # Print out the world map
    scene = [[["" for z in range(world.world_map_size_z)] for y in range(world.world_map_size_y)] for x in range(world.world_map_size_x)]

    for x in range(world.world_map_size_x):
        for y in range(world.world_map_size_y):
            for z in range(world.world_map_size_z):
                scene[x][y][z] = world.get_location(x, y, z).get_description()

    for x in range(world.world_map_size_x):
        for y in range(world.world_map_size_y):
            for z in range(world.world_map_size_z):
                print(scene[x][y][z], end="")
            print("")
    print("")


def run(descriptions):
    """Draws the scene of the described map. Public entry point of this unit."""
    render_scene(World(descriptions))
```

In this refactored code, I have decomposed the complex nested container into simpler ones. The `World` class constructor has been simplified by separating the logic of building the world map and getting the world map size. This makes the code easier to read and understand.

